\section{Conclusion}
In this paper, we address the discrepancy between CLIP’s semantic invariance and the perceptual requirements of NR-IQA via the Cross-modal Perception Alignment Adapter (CMPA), which mitigates perceptual submergence by decoupling quality cues through low-dimensional manifold projection. This framework is further supported by Residual-enhanced Perceptual Downscaling (RPD) to preserve high-frequency fidelity during mandatory resizing to get perception-consistent input. Extensive benchmarks demonstrate that our approach offers a competitive, parameter-efficient solution, highlighting that manifold-aware decoupling and input fidelity are essential for adapting foundation models to fine-grained perceptual tasks. This work represents a meaningful step toward more robust and perception-aware VLM adaptation.

